{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Mei, sorry Excel is getting in your way. Your company account looks healthy: it's active, not locked, and has no failed sign-ins. This message usually means Office has lost track of who's signed in. Please try these steps: 1) In Excel, go to File > Account and click Sign out. 2) Close all Office apps (Excel, Word, Outlook, Teams). 3) Reopen Excel and sign in with your company email, mei.chen@brightline.example, not a personal Microsoft account. 4) If it still says Unlicensed, go to Windows Settings > Accounts > Access work or school, remove any old or duplicate accounts, then restart your laptop and sign in again. If you've signed in with your company email and still see Unlicensed Product, reply and we'll pass it to our licensing team to check that your license is assigned. One more thing: your mailbox is about 99% full, so please delete or archive some old email soon so incoming mail doesn't stop.",
  "internal_note": "Ticket: Excel shows 'Unlicensed Product' and won't allow edits. Checked lookup_user (user_id 9): account active, 0 failed logins, not blocked, must_change_password false, password last set 2026-09-09. Device BL-LT-0109. No lockout or reset issue found. Sent KB-026 steps: sign out and back in with company email, remove duplicate work/school accounts, restart. Waiting on the user. If she signs in correctly and still sees Unlicensed, escalate to Tier 2 for a license assignment check (per KB-026). Side note: mailbox is at 99.4% (50890/51200 MB). User has been told.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-026"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
